# Base URL for the Node/Express API and Socket.IO server.
#
# Local dev: leave blank or omit — the client falls back to
#   http://localhost:3001 (or http://<page-hostname>:3001 if you load the
#   site from a phone on the same LAN).
#
# Production: set this to the public URL of your server, including the
# protocol (https://) so socket.io upgrades to WSS.
#   VITE_API_URL=https://api.kingcards.com
VITE_API_URL=

# ── Voice chat / WebRTC ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TURN relay used by the voice mesh when two peers can't connect
# directly. Without TURN, players on different networks (e.g. one on
# home Wi-Fi, one on cellular) usually CAN NOT establish audio — they'll
# appear in the voice roster but you won't hear them.
#
# How to get a TURN server (pick one):
#
# 1. Self-host coturn on the same VPS that runs the game server.
#    apt install coturn  → /etc/turnserver.conf:
#         listening-port=3478
#         tls-listening-port=5349
#         realm=kingcards.example.com
#         fingerprint
#         lt-cred-mech
#         user=kingcards:replace-with-strong-password
#         no-loopback-peers
#         no-multicast-peers
#    Open UDP/TCP 3478 + 5349 in the firewall, point your DNS at it,
#    then set the three vars below.
#
# 2. Use a managed TURN service (Twilio NTS, Metered, Xirsys,
#    Cloudflare Calls). Sign up, generate credentials, paste them here.
#
# `VITE_TURN_URLS` is a comma-separated list of one-or-more URLs. Mix
# transports (UDP / TCP / TLS) for the best chance of getting through
# strict firewalls:
#   VITE_TURN_URLS=turn:turn.kingcards.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turn:turn.kingcards.example.com:3478?transport=tcp,turns:turn.kingcards.example.com:5349?transport=tcp
#   VITE_TURN_USERNAME=kingcards
#   VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=replace-with-strong-password
VITE_TURN_URLS=
VITE_TURN_USERNAME=
VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=
